ABSTRACT = (
    "Online auction platforms are increasingly used for consumer and specialty goods trading, yet many deployments still struggle with listing trust, concurrent bidding integrity, payment settlement risk, and operational visibility for administrators. "
    "This project designs and implements Bid On, a next-generation web-based e-auction management system that combines moderated seller listings, real-time bidding, compliance gates for know-your-customer and bank verification, and an escrow-oriented payment workflow. "
    "The system follows a modular three-tier architecture: a Next.js progressive web frontend, an Express and Prisma application programming interface, and a PostgreSQL relational database hosted on Render. "
    "Socket.IO is used to propagate bid updates with low latency, while Stripe Checkout is integrated for winner payments with funds recorded in an escrow ledger prior to release or dispute resolution. "
    "The methodology covers requirements analysis mapped to an explicit functional and non-functional catalogue, logical and physical database design, secure authentication using JSON Web Tokens with httpOnly cookies, and deployment automation from GitHub. "
    "Evaluation is based on functional verification against the requirements chart, live deployment smoke tests, and critical code-level hardening of bidding concurrency, authentication session refresh, and payment confirmation. "
    "Results show that the implemented platform supports the primary auction lifecycle from moderated listing through live bidding and checkout, while remaining transparent about limitations such as free-tier cold starts, ephemeral upload storage, and the absence of automated seller payout via Stripe Connect. "
    "The work contributes a reproducible full-stack reference architecture for secure regional e-auctions and a complete academic documentation pack including editable diagrams and independent database artefacts."
)
